From f0a14bd347660791d6d73554a5a3ff6caed4cb03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StefanVonRanda <16295563+StefanVonRanda@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 08:58:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Add transparent PNG output (--rgba) and fused-qkv LoRAs

paralaif/MOSS-Image-Alpha-LoRA teaches MOSS-Image to put an alpha signal
into its latents; trmz/flux2-klein-alpha's RGBA VAE (same latent space and
batch-norm stats as FLUX.2's) decodes it to a fourth channel. moss-mlx's
decoder was hard-wired to 3 output channels, and the LoRA (trained with
ai-toolkit) adapts a fused layers.N.proj.qkv that MOSS's DiT keeps as
separate q, k, v, so apply_lora failed on it.

- vae.py: Decoder takes out_channels from the VAE config; rgba_vae_dir()
  fetches the RGBA VAE (config + weights) from the Hub on first use.
- pipeline.py: Request.rgba decodes with that VAE (the pipeline reloads the
  decoder only when the kind changes); inpainting pads its kept pixels with
  opaque alpha.
- lora.py: a qkv adapter on a block without qkv wraps q, k and v with the
  shared down and each one's third of up.
- cli.py: --rgba.

Checked: pytest 35 passed (qkv split equals the fused delta; a 4-channel
toy decoder). Real run, compact tier, 8-step DMD + moss_alpha_v1, CFG 1,
"A small red fox wizard with a glass potion bottle, full body, isolated on
a transparent background": RGBA PNG, 77 % of pixels alpha < 10, 19 % > 245,
the bottle translucent on a checkerboard; 38 s, 9.8 GB peak footprint.
Not checked: rgba together with inpainting on real weights.
The alpha LoRA is licensed for non-commercial research only.
